{"kind":"propose","text":"ADOPTION PROPOSAL — COMPOSITE KINDS, PHASE ONE: ADOPT `submit`, DEFER `undertake`.\n\nThe composite-kinds note merged at 38ed66b2 and is live at notes/2026-08-20-composite-kinds.md under artifact 5380fcf1, which this proposal rests on. That note was specification only and said so in its own front matter: adoption is a separate ratified proposal. This is that proposal. It enacts one of the two composites and defers the other, for the reason the note's own migration section gives.\n\nDECISION\n\n1. Adopt `submit` as a kind, exactly as the note's Definition section specifies it: fields present(path), present(commit), present(conditions) and optional type(to, actor-ref); basis count({promise, undertake}, 1..1); satisfier none; render artifact; staleness propagates; lifecycle none. A submit is the claimant pointing at the exact head that satisfies the claim, saying which conditions were met and which gates were run, and standing as the request for review.\n\n2. Widen the basis constraint on `promise` from count(1..1, request) to count({request, submit}, 1..1), with the corresponding widening of the fold's promise check. This is what lets a reviewer claim a review by promising on the submit directly, which is the whole of the saving on the review side. The `undertake` arm of that constraint is NOT adopted here.\n\n3. Turn the two compiled-name checks in `gs review`'s validateReview into render and lifecycle tests: the primary citation becomes any standing statement whose declared render is artifact, and the review promise's unique basis becomes any standing statement of lifecycle request or kind submit. reviewBasis.Request then holds the submit event and the verdict rests on {promise, submit, artifacts...} exactly as it rests on {promise, request, artifacts...} today.\n\n4. Make ui/src/lib/spine.ts read declared lifecycle and render rather than compiled kind names, so a submit is drawn with its label instead of falling through both branches. The note argues this is what the presentation contract asks for anyway.\n\n5. Do NOT adopt `undertake` now. Its lifecycle value does not exist in the evaluator, validateDefinition closes the lifecycle set to none/request/promise/report, and a room cannot ratify a new lifecycle value. It arrives as a fold activation judged by the incumbent, not as a ratification, and the note recommends shipping submit first and measuring. I agree, and I would rather measure one composite honestly than ship two and be unable to attribute the difference.\n\nOPEN QUESTIONS THIS SETTLES\n\nThe note left six open. Three bear on `submit` and are settled here by adopting the note's own answers, with the reasoning restated so a ratifier is not made to go and read for it.\n\nQ2, does `to` belong on submit: yes, and optional. Naming a reviewer is a courtesy, not a constraint the fold should enforce, and this workroom already behaves that way — unclaimed reviews are rerouted within minutes by whichever agent is free, which is exactly what happened to the two re-review requests planner picked up under offer 71cd3968 yesterday. When `to` is absent, any actor other than the submitter may claim the review.\n\nQ3, one submit per head or one per changed path: one per head, with plain artifacts carrying the other paths. The conditions of satisfaction are a property of the head, not of a file. Per-path submits would repeat the conditions once per path and would make the co-signing bound on merge harder to read, not easier.\n\nQ4, should review independence move into the fold: no, not in this phase. It stays a CLI guard. Moving it would make independence a fold concept, which it currently is not, and that is a larger change than this phase should carry. Revisit it with `undertake`, where a self-claim makes the question sharper.\n\nQ1, Q5 and Q6 all belong to `undertake` or beyond it and are untouched. Q6 in particular — that the expensive failure is a request refiled or abandoned rather than one claimed too slowly, on 180 request retirements against 34 promise retirements and 36% of commitments never closing — is a bigger finding than either composite, and neither composite addresses it. It should get its own investigation rather than being smuggled into this one.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT CHANGE\n\ngs merge is unchanged: it reads the approval and the artifacts it cites, tests Render == RenderArtifact, matches body.commit against the candidate, and derives its succession plan from reviewedPaths, and a submit is an artifact to all four. The co-signing bound is unchanged and must be re-checked at implementation time — a head publishing one submit plus five plain artifacts still needs all six named in gs review --artifact. Succession is unchanged, which is why submit declares render: artifact rather than a render class of its own; cmd/gs/succession.go republishes the mainline successor as a plain artifact carrying no conditions and no promise basis, so the composite appears on the branch side of a merge and never on the mainline side. No record schema version changes, because a kind is a body field, and no existing record is reinterpreted. ui/src/lib/rows.ts needs no change at all, because it builds every work row from projection.commitments and reads no kind name.\n\nDEPLOYMENT ORDER\n\nThe one this workroom has already been bitten by, and it is not optional: restart the resident sequencer and every MCP adapter on the new binary BEFORE rebuilding the CLI. An old fold refuses records a new CLI writes, and rules both composites undefined-kind, which is the typed and honest verdict but stops work while it lasts.\n\nWHAT SATISFIES THIS PROPOSAL\n\nRatification, then one implementation lane covering items 1 through 4, carried through independent exact-head review before merge. The measurement that decides `undertake` is a separate act after submit has been in use long enough to compare against the 2026-08-19 baseline of roughly 240 durable records a day.\n\nI am the author of the note this adopts, so I do not ratify it. Hugh: this is the decision half of your 2026-08-20 composite-kinds priority."}